window.docContents[239].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Cauchy-problems":{"contents":"A Cauchy problem prescribes a solution and some of its derivatives on a\nhypersurface  and asks the equation to do the rest. For an equation\nof order  the natural data are the values of  and its first \nnormal derivatives along . Two questions come before any existence\ntheorem. Do these data, together with the equation, pin down every derivative\nof  on ? And if they do, does the resulting Taylor series\nconverge?\n\nThe one-dimensional wave equation  shows how the first\nquestion can fail. On the line  the data are  and\n. Differentiating them in  gives , , and\n on the line, and the equation then supplies the one missing\nsecond derivative, . On the line  the same procedure\nbreaks down. Differentiating the first derivatives along the line gives\n and , hence their difference\n. The equation prescribes exactly that difference, so it\neither repeats what the data already say or contradicts them, and in neither\ncase does it determine the three second derivatives.\nThe difference is visible in the principal symbol: for a normal\n it is , which equals  for\n and vanishes for . A hypersurface is\nnoncharacteristic when the principal symbol is nonzero on its normals.\n\nEven when every derivative is determined, the Taylor series need not\nconverge. Kovalevskaya's example is the heat equation  with\n. The line  is characteristic for the heat\noperator, whose principal symbol  vanishes at , but\nthe equation is solved for , so every time derivative is still\ndetermined formally: . At the origin\n, so the formal solution is\n\n\n\nwhose terms grow roughly like  and diverge for every , although\n is analytic on the whole real line. The wave equation with the same\ndatum and  has the solution\n, and at  its series\n converges for .\n\n\n\nThe figure plots both series. The wave partial sums settle onto\n inside the unit interval, while at each plotted time the size of\nthe -th term of the heat series first shrinks and then grows without\nbound. The Cauchy–Kovalevskaya theorem says that the divergence cannot happen\nfor a first-order system solved for its time derivative with analytic\ncoefficients and data.  treats\nnoncharacteristic surfaces in §4.6.1 and the analytic theorem in §4.6.3.\nThe determination of derivatives is proved for smooth solutions of a\nquasilinear scalar equation, with no analyticity assumption. The convergence\nproof runs on\nMathlib's multivariable formal power series, with the formal solution\nconstructed by recursion in the time degree, over any commutative\n-algebra, before any estimate is made.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009PDE Problems and Differential Operators","header":"3.2.4. Cauchy problems","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Cauchy-problems"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--Burgers-Equation--Explicit-and-Riemann-solutions":{"contents":"For affine initial data , the characteristic construction gives\n. The accompanying problem imposes the PDE on\n and records the initial trace.\nWhen  this domain also contains the branch  beyond the\nsingular time.\n\nFor affine data the footpoint equation  is linear in ,\nso the characteristic construction can be carried out in closed form and the\nJacobian denominator becomes .\n\nFor , the explicit profilesatisfies  wherever  and , and\nrecovers the affine initial trace. The pre-shock part of this domain is\n, : all forward time for , and\n for .\n\nSplit the problem into its evolution equation and initial condition.\nOn the specified domain, the denominator is nonzero, so the explicit time and\nspace derivative formulas reduce  to a rational expression whose\ntwo terms cancel. On the time-zero slice, direct simplification gives\n.\n\nWhen  the gradient  of the affine profile blows up at\n, the simplest instance of crossing characteristics. The Riemann\nproblem, with constant states  and  separated by a jump at the\norigin, is the model for what happens afterwards, and for the flux\n the scalar jump conditions become explicit: the Rankine–Hugoniot\nsecant speed is the average of the two states.\n\nFor Burgers data with , the speed  satisfies the\nRankine–Hugoniot relation and the Lax compressive shock inequalities.\n\nFor the flux , the Rankine–Hugoniot quotient is\n. Since\n, their arithmetic mean lies strictly between them. Because the\ncharacteristic speed is , these are exactly the two Lax\ninequalities.\n\nLax admissibility is a statement about the speed alone. Whether the two-state\nprofile traveling at that speed solves the equation is a separate question,\nanswered by the general planar-shock theorem.\n\nFor every pair of states , the Burgers shock profileis a weak solution of  on all of spacetime. No ordering of\nthe states is required: the expansive profile with  is a weak\nsolution as well.\n\nThe profile is the scalar planar shock with left state , right state\n and speed . For the flux  this speed\nsatisfies\n,\nwhich is the Rankine–Hugoniot relation for every pair of states, so the\ngeneral planar-shock theorem applies.\n\nOne entropy pair already separates the two orderings. Take\n and , so . Using the\nright-minus-left jump convention and  gives\n\n\n\nThis is the coefficient of the interface measure in\n. It is negative for a compressive\nshock and positive for an expansion shock, so the expansion shock already\nfails this one test. Admissibility requires the sign for every convex\nentropy pair.\n\nThe entropy inequality is what survives of a small viscosity. With\n in place of the inviscid law, the Cole–Hopf\nsubstitution  turns the Riemann\nproblem into a heat equation for , and the solution can be written\nwith two error functions. The figure evaluates it for three values of\n. For expansive data the viscous profiles approach the fan and\nstay away from the expansion shock, although both are weak solutions of the\nlimit equation; for compressive data they steepen into the Lax shock. Each\nviscous solution satisfies  for convex , and letting\n leaves exactly the entropy inequality. The\nvanishing-viscosity\ntheorem for systems proves this passage to the limit for bounded classical\nviscous solutions that are continuous up to  and converge almost\neverywhere. The viscous solutions in the figure start from the discontinuous\nRiemann datum, so for them the limit is a computation.\n\n\n\nLet  and set . The two-state profile with jump\nalong  is an entropy weak solution on all of spacetime. For every\n entropy pair  with convex  and\n, it satisfies\n\nRankine–Hugoniot gives the weak equation. Integration by parts on the two\nsides of the shock expresses the entropy integral as\n, whereSince  is the midpoint, . Subtracting\n from the other factor leaves\n, because the derivative of a convex\nfunction is monotone. Thus , which gives the required sign for\nevery nonnegative test.\n\nFor , the entropy solution joins the constant states by the\nself-similar fan .\n\nFor , the profileis an entropy weak solution on the positive-time half-plane.\n\nSplit the half-plane into the two constant regions and the fan\n. The conservation law and every smooth entropy identity hold\nclassically in each region. The profile is continuous across both fan\nboundaries, so integration by parts creates no jump measure there. Summing\nthe three regional identities therefore gives the weak equation and the\nentropy inequality, in fact with equality inside the fan.\n\nThe shock and the rarefaction cover the two orderings of the states, and\nselecting between them by the sign of  gives a single formula for\nevery pair. The new point is the initial trace.\n\nFor every pair of constant left and right states, the explicit Burgers\nRiemann profile selects the shock branch when  and the rarefaction\nbranch otherwise. It is an entropy weak solution for positive time and\nconverges strongly in  to the discontinuous Riemann\ndatum as .\n\nSplit on the order of the two states. The strict branch restricts the\nwhole-space entropy shock to positive time; the other branch uses the\nentropy rarefaction and almost-everywhere invariance. For each fixed\n, the profile equals the corresponding initial state once\n is small enough. Both the profile and the datum are bounded in\nabsolute value by . Their measurable difference is\ntherefore dominated by  on each bounded interval. Dominated\nconvergence gives the strong  trace.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Conservation Laws\u0009Burgers Equation","header":"7.5.2.7.1. Explicit and Riemann solutions","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--Burgers-Equation--Explicit-and-Riemann-solutions"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--Energy-estimates--Conservation-from-separate-compact-slices":{"contents":"Suppose only that each displacement  has compact support;\nits supporting set may depend on . Differentiating a whole-space\nintegral still needs control over a time neighborhood. For a \nreaction, the wave equation supplies a common support on every finite\nforward slab, which closes that step.\n\nThe following results concern the closed forward half-space. Write\n for the same energy integral using the differential within\n. At zero this uses the actual right\ntime derivative, and at positive times it agrees with the ordinary energy.\n\nLet , , and .\nSuppose  is  on the closed forward half-space, satisfies\n at positive times, and\n has compact support for every . ThenNo common spatial support, sign condition on , or assumption about\nnegative-time values is required.\n\nBaire category supplies a time interval with common compact displacement\nsupport. Since  is locally Lipschitz, finite propagation transports\nthat support across each finite forward slab. The local energy balance\ncan then be integrated on one fixed compact spatial region. A smooth\nextension across the initial face identifies the initial energy with the\nright differential; the answer is independent of the extension.\n\nFor a merely continuous reaction, this support-propagation argument is no\nlonger available in the same form. In low dimensions a direct estimate of\nthe flux at spatial infinity suffices.\n\nLet  and . Suppose\n at positive times and\n has compact support for each .\nIf  and  is  on the closed forward half-space, or\n and  is  there, thenThere is no sign or growth condition on .\n\nIn one dimension, characteristic transport bounds the first derivatives\nlocally uniformly in time. Separate compact slices make the endpoint flux\nvanish at each time, and dominated convergence removes the endpoints of a\nfinite rectangular energy balance. Local constancy then gives conservation\non the connected forward time interval.In two dimensions, the equation supplies uniform smallness of the potential\nat spatial infinity on each finite slab. Integrating the energy and\nmomentum balances leaves an annular stress term. The annulus area grows\nquadratically in its radius, while the cutoff's second derivatives decay\nquadratically. Their factors cancel, so the small potential tail makes\nthe remaining term vanish.\n\nLet , , and . Assume that for\nsome real ,  throughout a neighborhood of zero.\nIf  is  on the closed forward half-space, solves\n at positive times, and has compact\ndisplacement support separately at each , then\n\nAdding a suitable multiple of  to the cone energy makes it\nnonnegative on the attained value range. The potential's local lower bound\ncontrols this modification near zero; continuity controls it away from zero.\nGrönwall then propagates zero Cauchy data across backward cones, in both time\ndirections. Baire category gives a time near which all slices vanish outside\none ball, so the Cauchy data there have common compact support, and finite\npropagation carries it across each finite slab. The resulting common support\nallows the ordinary energy balance to be integrated.\n\nFor a merely continuous reaction in dimension , conservation is\nproved only under the quadratic lower bound, which is an additional\nhypothesis and not a consequence of continuity. The two-dimensional annular\nargument leaves a factor  in higher dimensions, and uniform\nsmallness of the potential tail gives no decay rate that cancels it.\n\nConservation alone says nothing about the size of the individual terms, since\n may be negative. Under  each of the three contributions is\nnonnegative and hence bounded by their sum, which conservation pins to the\ninitial energy. These are a priori bounds on a given solution.\n\nIf in addition , then the total energy and each of its normalized\nkinetic, spatial-gradient, and potential contributions are nonnegative, and\neach contribution is bounded by . Under the conservation hypotheses,\nthe same quantities at any later time are bounded by the initial energy\n.\n\nNonnegativity of  makes every term of the energy density nonnegative.\nEach individual integral is therefore bounded by their sum. Conservation\nreplaces that sum at time  by , giving the reference-time\nbounds.\n\nLocalizing the same computation to a ball that shrinks at unit speed gives the\nsemilinear analogue of the moving-ball energy identity of the linear chapter.\nThe potential contributes twice: inside the density being differentiated, and\nthrough the boundary swept out by the shrinking sphere.\n\nFor , a  potential, a globally  field, and , the\nderivative of the total energy on the shrinking ball\n is the sum of its local volume defect and lateral cone flux. If\nthe equation holds throughout that ball, the volume defect vanishes and only\nthe lateral fluxremains. Its sphere integral equals the classical lateral flux minus the\npotential energy swept out by the shrinking boundary. Both identities follow\nfrom the Reynolds transport and Gauss formulas on balls.\n\nReynolds transport differentiates the energy on the shrinking ball and\nproduces the swept-boundary term. Integrating the local balance converts the\nspatial divergence into a sphere flux by Gauss's theorem. On a solution the\nvolume residual vanishes, and separating the potential gives the displayed\nlateral flux.\n\nWith  the lateral flux is nonpositive by the same Cauchy–Schwarz step\nas in the linear case, and with  the zero state has zero potential\nenergy, so the cone energy of a solution with zero data on the base ball\ncannot leave zero. As in the global bounds, the sign condition makes the\nenergy control its parts; conservation itself did not need it.\n\nIn positive spatial dimension, suppose  is , nonnegative, and\n. If a globally  classical solution\nof  has zero displacement and velocity on the base\nball, then it vanishes in the corresponding open backward cone. This is\nEvans's theorem.\nFor the closed-cone theorem, require the data to vanish on the closed base\nball. The conclusion then includes the lateral boundary and vertex.\n\nThe proof is the energy argument for finite propagation (Evans, 2010,\np. 661).Monotonicity of cone energy. Define\n\non the shrinking ball . By the transport theorem (proved above),\n equals the lateral cone flux. The flux integrand is\n.\nBy Cauchy–Schwarz,Since  (with\n), the full integrand is nonpositive. Therefore .Zero initial energy. The zero data on the base ball give \n(using  for the potential term). Combined with monotonicity,\n for all .Vanishing of the solution. Zero kinetic-gradient energy forces\n and  almost everywhere in each shrinking ball.\nThe connected-component argument (integrating zero velocity along vertical\nsegments from the initial surface) gives  in the open cone. With\nclosed-base data, continuity extends the conclusion to the closed cone\nboundary. The formal proof takes a more general route: it adds a multiple of\n to the cone energy, which allows any  with  near\nzero, and applies Grönwall; for  the bound holds with .\n\nStability and uniqueness come from applying the energy method to the\ndifference  of two solutions. The difference satisfies a wave equation\nwith source , which the Lipschitz bound controls by ; to\nabsorb this term the difference energy is augmented by , and\nthe augmented quantity obeys a Grönwall inequality with rate .\n\nIf  is globally -Lipschitz and  solve\n, assume both fields are globally , every\naugmented difference-energy slice is integrable, and its time derivative\nhas an integrable dominator valid almost everywhere in space throughout a\ncommon time neighborhood of each time. Assume also that each fixed-time\ndifference-energy flux component has compact support. Then the augmented\ndifference energy satisfiesHere  includes the auxiliary coercive term ,\nwhich is unrelated to any primitive of .\n\nSubtract the equations. Lipschitz continuity bounds the nonlinear residual\nin the difference-energy identity and gives\n. A two-sided Grönwall argument yields the estimate\nfor arbitrary time order.\n\nThe augmented energy is half the sum of the three squared  norms.\nIn particular, . Taking square roots\nof the exponential estimate gives continuous dependence on the Cauchy data\nin the energy norm.\n\nThe exponential estimate controls the integral of the squared velocity\ndifference, spatial-gradient difference, and field difference by\n.\n\nFor the quadratic auxiliary potential , coercivity bounds the\nsum of the three squared difference terms by twice the augmented energy at\ntime . Compose this estimate with exponential stability of that energy\nbetween  and .\n\nEqual Cauchy data at one time make the augmented difference energy vanish\nthere, and the exponential bound then keeps it zero at every time. Because the\naugmented energy controls  on each slice and  is continuous,\nthis yields pointwise equality everywhere, under the same integrability and\ncompact-flux hypotheses as the stability estimate.\n\nLet  and  solve\n for the same globally Lipschitz . Ifthen  everywhere, under the analytic integrability and flux\nhypotheses used by the energy argument.\n\nEqual Cauchy data make the augmented difference energy zero at the reference\ntime. Exponential stability keeps it zero on every time slice, and coercivity\nthen forces the pointwise field difference to vanish.\n\nThe global statement needs integrability over whole slices. Working instead\nwith the quadratic cone energy of  on the shrinking ball removes every\nintegrability hypothesis, since continuous functions are integrable over\nbounded balls; the price is that the conclusion is confined to the backward\ncone, exactly as for the linear domain of dependence.\n\nLet , let  be globally -Lipschitz, and let\n. Fix a center  and times\n, and suppose both fields satisfythat is, inside the open backward cone with vertex . If\n and  on the closed base\nball , thenWith data agreeing only on the open base ball , the conclusion\nholds on the open cone , . No sign condition on\n is needed, and the only regularity hypothesis is that both fields are\nglobally ; this localizes the global uniqueness theorem above.\n\nLet  and consider the quadratic cone energythe semilinear cone energy of  for the potential . By the\nshrinking-ball transport theorem its derivative is the lateral cone flux plus\nthe volume term . Cauchy–Schwarz makes\nthe lateral flux nonpositive, and subtracting the two equations gives\n, so the Lipschitz bound controls the volume\nterm by . Grönwall's inequality yields\n, and equal Cauchy data give . Hence\nthe classical cone energy of  vanishes on , which forces \nthroughout the closed cone by the vanishing argument of the linear theory.\nThe open-cone version applies the closed-cone statement to a slightly smaller\ncone through each interior point.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves\u0009Nonlinear Wave Equations\u0009Energy estimates","header":"7.2.4.5.1. Conservation from separate compact slices","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--Energy-estimates--Conservation-from-separate-compact-slices"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Pressure-from-the-vorticity-equation":{"contents":"The pressure Poisson equation is one consequence of the momentum balance.\nConversely, in a force-free flow the momentum equation determines\n, so pressure can be reconstructed from the\nvelocity alone whenever that candidate field is a gradient. The compatibility\ncondition is exactly the vorticity equation.\n\nFor a spacetime velocity , the spatial\nvorticity two-form has componentsthe transpose, equivalently the negative, of the antisymmetric\nvelocity-gradient convention used for the Euler vorticity tensor. Its\ntransport-diffusion residual with viscosity  iswhere the deformation sum is the coordinate form of the Lie derivative of a\ntwo-form along . In three dimensions the vector vorticity is the curl of\neach time slice, ; for a \nvelocity its components are the cyclic two-form entries\n, , .\nNo incompressibility is built into these definitions.\n\nIn three dimensions an antisymmetric matrix has three independent entries, and\nfor  these are the components of . The two-form\nresidual and the vector residual introduced for Euler should therefore carry\nthe same information, and they do, up to a correction proportional to\n that disappears for incompressible flow.\n\nLet  be  and let  be a\nspacetime point at which . Then the two-form residual\nvanishes at  if and only if the vector residual does:The implication from the two-form equation to the vector equation needs only\n regularity.\n\nEach cyclic component of the two-form residual equals the corresponding\ncomponent of the vector residual plus the correction\n; this is an exact identity obtained by expanding\nthe deformation sum and regrouping it in terms of the curl components. At a\npoint where the divergence vanishes, the correction disappears and the three\ncyclic components of  are the three components of the vector residual.\nThe remaining components of  follow from the antisymmetry of .\n\nThe connection with pressure runs through the antisymmetrized derivative of the\ncandidate field . Differentiating  and\ncommuting mixed derivatives reproduces, term by term, the two-form residual\nwith the opposite sign, so the vorticity equation is precisely the statement\nthat  has a symmetric Jacobian.\n\nWrite  for the pressure-gradient candidate: the\nforce-free Navier–Stokes momentum equation is . If  is\n, or merely  at the spacetime point , then at The candidate depends only on the velocity germ: if  on a\nneighborhood of , then  on a neighborhood of . It is\n near every point at which  is .\n\nExpand  componentwise and differentiate. The antisymmetrised\nderivative of  is , that of the\nLaplacian is , and the convective term\n contributes  plus\nthe deformation sum\n after commuting\nthe mixed derivatives, which is where third derivatives are used. Collecting\nterms gives minus the two-form residual. Since  is built from finitely\nmany partial derivatives at each point, it is determined by the germ of\n; the local statements follow by replacing  with a compactly\nsupported  field that agrees with it near .\n\nA  field with symmetric Jacobian on an open convex set is a gradient, by\nintegration along segments from a base point. Applied slice by slice to\n, this converts the vorticity equation into the existence of a pressure\nclosing the force-free momentum equation.\n\nLet  be open and convex, let \nbe any set of times, and let  be  at\nevery point  with  and , satisfying the two-form\nvorticity equation  there. Then there is a pressure\n such that for all  and\n, the slice  has gradient  at  andFor  this reconstructs the force-free Euler pressure,\n. In three dimensions the two-form hypothesis may be\nreplaced by  together with the vector vorticity equation\n for\n, again for Navier–Stokes and for Euler.The pressure is selected slice by slice, with no condition in time, and \nmay exclude a blow-up time: only local regularity at the selected points is\nused. The reconstructed momentum equation is force free\nbecause the vorticity equation carries no forcing term.\n\nAt each selected time the candidate  is  on , and\nby the antisymmetry identity its Jacobian is symmetric exactly because the\nvorticity residual vanishes. On an open convex set, a  field with\nsymmetric derivative is a gradient: integrating along segments from a base\npoint produces a potential whose gradient is the field. Choosing such a\npotential for every  defines , and rewriting\n gives the momentum equation. The\nthree-dimensional statements first convert the vector vorticity equation into\nthe two-form equation using incompressibility.\n\nPressure reconstruction completes the pointwise picture: on the selected slices\nthe velocity alone fixes the pressure gradient. Estimates, on the other hand,\ncome from integrated quantities, first among them the kinetic energy, and these\nhave to be defined so that they make sense for weak velocities whose\nderivatives exist only distributionally.\n\nThe kinetic energy isand we also define the initial-data energy, enstrophy, and two forms of viscous\ndissipation. The classical dissipation differentiates  pointwise; the weak\nquantity integrates\nthe square of a separately supplied distributional-gradient witness.  Keeping\nthese definitions distinct avoids applying totalized classical derivatives\nto an energy-class velocity.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow\u0009Stokes and Incompressible Navier–Stokes","header":"8.5.1.4. Pressure from the vorticity equation","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Pressure-from-the-vorticity-equation"}});